<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Internationale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words June <a href="/years/1871">1871</a> (an earlier version September <a href="/years/1870">1870</a>); music <a href="/years/1888">1888</a>, first performed in Lille in July <a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as L'Internationale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eugène Pottier, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a> who had attended its congress of 1864 and a member of the Paris Commune, wrote the French words in June 1871, after the French army crushed the Commune on 28 May and before he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and then, from 1873 to 1881, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-internationale.jpg" alt="The Internationale" width="330" height="534" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Degeyter (1848–1932) with the lyric written in 1871 by Eugène Pottier (1816–1887) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L'Internationale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The movement also left a culture of its own: banners, brass bands, choirs and sports clubs, cooperative shops, workers’ colleges and newspapers, and songs such as “<b>The Internationale</b>”, written by Eugène Pottier in 1871 and set to music by Pierre De Geyter in 1888.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The composer Pablo Sorozábal recalled that when his orchestra played it, the anarchists and syndicalists stood and gave the anarchist salute, hands locked together over their heads, while the socialists and communists stayed seated, and that the reverse happened when the orchestra played &quot;<b>The Internationale</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-las-barricadas">A las Barricadas</a></li><li><blockquote>The historian Oliver Rathkolb describes it as created as a counterweight to &quot;<b>The Internationale</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Comintern was dissolved in 1943 so that the Soviet Union could keep its alliance with the other Allies of World War II, a new anthem was needed to replace &quot;<b>The Internationale</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li><li><blockquote>Loudspeakers in towns and villages played it at dawn and dusk, the Central People's Broadcasting Station opened each day with it on a set of bronze bells cast in the Warring States period, and broadcasts commonly began with it and closed with &quot;<b>The Internationale</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li><li><blockquote>…a rally in Leningrad against Lord Curzon's ultimatum to Soviet Russia, which gave one of its lines new weight, and Nadezhda Krupskaya, Lenin's wife, named it in a letter to a school for blind pupils in the Vologda region as one of her favourite songs, beside &quot;<b>The Internationale</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest</a></li></ul></section>
